(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-07"] = {"mth-11-07-m01":{"idea":"Use addition when the choices are alternatives. Only one of them is done, never both together.","steps":["The job can be done in m ways, or in n other ways.","The two kinds of ways cannot happen together.","So we count all the ways of both kinds: m + n.","Example: 3 buses or 2 trains give 3 + 2 = 5 ways."],"others":"The choice m − n would remove ways instead of adding them. The choice m × n is for steps done one after another. The choice m/n has no meaning for counting ways.","trap":"'Or' means add. 'And then' means multiply.","answer":"m + n ways"},"mth-11-07-m02":{"idea":"Use multiplication when the steps follow each other. Each way of the first step goes with each way of the second.","steps":["The first step has m ways.","For each of them, the second step has n ways.","So the total is m × n.","Example: 3 shirts and 4 trousers give 3 × 4 = 12 outfits."],"others":"The choice mⁿ is for n steps each with m ways. The choice m + n is for alternatives. The choice m − n has no use in counting.","answer":"m × n ways"},"mth-11-07-m03":{"idea":"Going from A to B and then B to C are two steps one after another, so we multiply.","steps":["A to B has 3 routes.","For each of them, B to C has 4 routes.","Total = 3 × 4 = 12."],"others":"The value 7 is 3 + 4, which is right for alternatives only. The values 64 and 81 are 4³ and 3⁴, which have no link with this problem.","trap":"Steps in a row: multiply.","answer":"12"},"mth-11-07-m04":{"idea":"The invariance principle says the order in which we count does not change the total.","steps":["Suppose we choose a pencil and an eraser.","Choosing the pencil first gives p × e ways.","Choosing the eraser first gives e × p ways, the same number.","So the steps may be taken in any order."],"others":"The choice that says the count changes with the order is false, because multiplication is the same either way. The choice about sum only is false, since we multiply for steps. The choice that says every problem has the same count is false, since each problem has its own count.","answer":"the number of ways does not change if the same things are counted in a different order"},"mth-11-07-m05":{"idea":"0! is defined to be 1. This keeps the formulas for nPr and nCr working at the edges.","steps":["By definition, 0! = 1.","Why: nCn = n!/(n! × 0!) must be 1, since there is one way to choose all n objects.","That works only if 0! = 1."],"others":"The value 0 would make nCn divide by zero. The words 'infinity' and 'undefined' are wrong, because 0! is simply defined as 1.","trap":"0! is 1, not 0.","answer":"1"},"mth-11-07-m06":{"idea":"n! means the product of all whole numbers from n down to 1.","steps":["5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1.","5 × 4 = 20, and 20 × 3 = 60.","60 × 2 = 120, and 120 × 1 = 120."],"others":"The value 25 is 5² and 60 is 5 × 4 × 3, which stops too early. The value 720 is 6!, one step too far.","trap":"Quick check: 5! = 120 and 6! = 720.","answer":"120"},"mth-11-07-m07":{"idea":"The factorial of n is n times the factorial of the number just before it.","steps":["n! = n × (n − 1) × (n − 2) × … × 1.","The part after n is (n − 1)!.","So n! = n × (n − 1)!.","Example: 7! = 7 × 6!."],"others":"The other forms add, subtract or divide, and none of them gives the product n × (n − 1) × … × 1. For n = 4 they give wrong numbers: 4! = 24, but 4 + 3! = 10.","trap":"Factorial is a product, never a sum.","answer":"n × (n − 1)!"},"mth-11-07-m08":{"idea":"nPr counts the ordered ways to pick r objects out of n different ones.","steps":["The first place can be filled in n ways.","The second place in n − 1 ways, and so on.","The r-th place in n − r + 1 ways.","The product is n!/(n − r)!. Example: 5P3 = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60."],"others":"The formula with r! in the bottom is nCr, where order does not matter. The formula n!/r! is not any standard count. The formula with (n + r)! on the bottom has the wrong sign.","answer":"n!/(n − r)!"},"mth-11-07-m09":{"idea":"Making numbers from digits is an ordered arrangement, so it is a permutation.","steps":["There are 3 places, and 5 digits to choose from, with no repeat.","The first place has 5 choices, the second 4, the third 3.","Total = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 (this is 5P3)."],"others":"The value 125 is 5³, which is the count when digits can repeat. The value 10 is 5C3, which ignores the order. The value 120 is 5!, which uses all 5 digits.","trap":"No repeat: the choices go down. With repeat: 5³.","answer":"60"},"mth-11-07-m10":{"idea":"When letters repeat, divide by the factorial of each repeat. Swapping equal letters gives the same word.","steps":["LEADER has 6 letters, and E appears twice.","If all letters were different: 6! = 720 arrangements.","Swapping the two E's does not give a new word, so divide by 2!.","720/2 = 360."],"others":"The value 720 forgets the repeated E. The value 180 divides by 4, not 2. The value 120 is 5!, which is not related here.","answer":"360"},"mth-11-07-m11":{"idea":"This is the same idea: total letters factorial, divided by the factorial of each repeat.","steps":["MISSISSIPPI has 11 letters: M once, I four times, S four times, P twice.","If all were different: 11! arrangements.","Divide by 4! for I, 4! for S and 2! for P.","Total = 11!/(4! 4! 2!) = 34 650."],"others":"The choice with only 4! 4! forgets the P's. The choice with 4! alone forgets the second letter that repeats four times. The choice 11! ignores all repeats.","trap":"Divide by the factorial of every letter that repeats.","answer":"11!/(4! 4! 2!)"},"mth-11-07-m12":{"idea":"Tie the two persons that must stand together into one block. Count the block as a single unit.","steps":["The block and the other 3 persons make 4 units.","4 units can stand in a row in 4! = 24 ways.","Inside the block, the two persons can swap: 2 ways.","Total = 24 × 2 = 48."],"others":"The value 24 forgets the swap inside the block. The value 120 is 5!, with no condition. The value 96 doubles the right answer by mistake.","trap":"Do not forget the 2! for the order inside the block.","answer":"48"},"mth-11-07-m13":{"idea":"In a circle, there is no first place. So we fix one object and arrange the rest.","steps":["Fix one object at any seat.","The other n − 1 objects can be arranged in (n − 1)! ways.","So n objects in a circle: (n − 1)! ways.","Example: 5 people at a round table: 4! = 24 ways, not 120."],"others":"The value n! counts a line, which treats every rotation as new. The value (n + 1)! is too large. The value n!/2 is for a necklace that can be flipped, not for a table.","trap":"Circle: (n − 1)!. Line: n!.","answer":"(n − 1)!"},"mth-11-07-m14":{"idea":"A combination is a selection. Only the members matter, not the order.","steps":["Choosing a team of 3 players from 7: the order of the three does not matter.","This is a combination.","Arranging 3 players in a line is different: that is a permutation."],"others":"The arrangement in a fixed order is a permutation. The idea of repeating objects as we like is not the meaning. A combination is never larger than the permutation, since nCr = nPr/r!.","answer":"a selection of objects in which the order does not matter"},"mth-11-07-m15":{"idea":"nCr is the number of ways to choose r objects out of n different ones, ignoring the order.","steps":["Each choice of r objects can be arranged in r! orders.","So nPr = nCr × r!.","Therefore nCr = nPr/r! = n!/(r!(n − r)!).","Example: 7C3 = 7 × 6 × 5/(3 × 2 × 1) = 35."],"others":"The formula n!/(n − r)! is nPr, which counts the order. The formula n!/r! is not a standard count. The formula with r! on top is upside down.","answer":"n!/(r!(n − r)!)"},"mth-11-07-m16":{"idea":"Choosing r objects to take is the same as choosing n − r objects to leave behind.","steps":["Each selection of r objects leaves behind a group of n − r objects.","So the number of selections is the same: nCr = nC(n − r).","Example: 10C3 = 10C7 = 120."],"others":"nCr = nPr is false, because nPr is larger by the factor r!. nC(r − n) has a negative number, which is meaningless. nCr = n!/r! is also not true, since it lacks the (n − r)! part.","answer":"nCr = nC(n − r)"},"mth-11-07-m17":{"idea":"Two combinations with the same n are equal in two cases: same r, or r values that add up to n.","steps":["We know nCr = nC(n − r).","So nCx = nCy when x = y, or when x = n − y.","Since x is not equal to y, we must have x + y = n.","Example: 12Cx = 12C4 with x not 4 gives x = 8."],"others":"The choice x = y is excluded by the question. The choices xy = n and x − y = n do not follow from the symmetry rule. Test: 12C8 = 12C4, but 8 × 4 = 32 and 8 − 4 = 4, neither is 12.","answer":"x + y = n"},"mth-11-07-m18":{"idea":"We write nC2 as a formula in n and solve the equation.","steps":["nC2 = n(n − 1)/2.","So n(n − 1)/2 = 45, which gives n(n − 1) = 90.","90 = 10 × 9, so n = 10.","Check: 10 × 9/2 = 45."],"others":"The value 45 gives 45C2, which is far larger. The value 11 gives 11C2 = 55. The value 9 gives 9C2 = 36.","answer":"10"},"mth-11-07-m19":{"idea":"Choosing a committee is a selection, so the order does not matter. Use a combination.","steps":["Number of ways = 7C3.","7C3 = 7!/(3! × 4!).","= (7 × 6 × 5)/(3 × 2 × 1) = 210/6 = 35."],"others":"The value 210 is 7P3, which counts the order of the three persons also. The value 343 is 7³, which is for choices with repeat. The value 21 is 7C2.","trap":"Committee or team: no order, so nCr.","answer":"35"},"mth-11-07-m20":{"idea":"Any two vertices can be joined by a line. Some of those lines are sides, and the rest are diagonals.","steps":["Pairs of vertices = nC2, so nC2 lines in all.","Of these, n lines are the sides of the polygon.","Diagonals = nC2 − n = n(n − 3)/2.","Example: n = 10 gives 45 − 10 = 35."],"others":"The choice nC2 counts the sides also. The choice nC2 + n adds the sides instead of removing them. The choice n(n − 1) counts each pair twice, and also includes the sides.","trap":"Remember to subtract the n sides.","answer":"nC2 − n"},"mth-11-07-m21":{"idea":"'At least 2 women' means 2, 3 or 4 women. Count each case and add.","steps":["2 women and 2 men: 4C2 × 5C2 = 6 × 10 = 60.","3 women and 1 man: 4C3 × 5C1 = 4 × 5 = 20.","4 women: 4C4 = 1.","Total = 60 + 20 + 1 = 81."],"others":"The value 126 is all teams of 4 from 9, with no condition. The value 45 is the number of teams with fewer than 2 women. The value 60 counts only the case of exactly 2 women.","trap":"Check: 126 − 45 = 81.","answer":"81"},"mth-11-07-m22":{"idea":"The key question is whether a different order gives a different result.","steps":["Arranging, ranking or forming a number: order matters, so use a permutation.","Choosing a team or a committee: order does not matter, so use a combination.","So we ask: does the order of the chosen objects matter?"],"others":"The number of men and women, the colour of objects and the size of the numbers have no effect on whether order matters. They do not decide between permutation and combination.","answer":"whether the order of the chosen objects matters"},"mth-11-07-n01":{"idea":"Forming a number from digits with no repeat is an ordered arrangement. Multiply the choices place by place.","steps":["There are 3 places and 5 digits, with no repeat.","First place: 5 choices. Second place: 4 choices. Third place: 3 choices.","Total = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 (this is 5P3)."],"trap":"With repeat allowed, the answer would be 5³ = 125.","answer":"60"},"mth-11-07-n02":{"idea":"Repeated letters must be divided out. For each repeated letter, divide by its factorial.","steps":["MISSISSIPPI has 11 letters: I four times, S four times, P twice, M once.","Number = 11!/(4! × 4! × 2!).","11! = 39 916 800, and 4! × 4! × 2! = 24 × 24 × 2 = 1152.","39 916 800/1152 = 34 650."],"trap":"Check by choosing places: 330 × 35 × 3 = 34 650.","answer":"34650"},"mth-11-07-n03":{"idea":"A committee has no order, so this is a combination.","steps":["Number of ways = 7C3 = 7!/(3! × 4!).","= (7 × 6 × 5)/(3 × 2 × 1).","= 210/6 = 35."],"trap":"7P3 = 210 would count the order too, which is wrong here.","answer":"35"},"mth-11-07-n04":{"idea":"'At least 2 women' means 2, 3 or 4 women. Count each case and add.","steps":["2 women and 2 men: 4C2 × 5C2 = 6 × 10 = 60.","3 women and 1 man: 4C3 × 5C1 = 4 × 5 = 20.","4 women: 4C4 = 1.","Total = 60 + 20 + 1 = 81."],"trap":"Check: all teams 9C4 = 126, minus teams with fewer than 2 women (45), gives 81.","answer":"81"},"mth-11-07-n05":{"idea":"A 4-digit number cannot start with 0. So fill the first place carefully, then the rest.","steps":["The digits are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, with no repeat.","First place: 5 choices (any digit except 0).","Second place: 5 choices (0 is now allowed, one digit used).","Third place: 4 choices. Fourth place: 3 choices.","Total = 5 × 5 × 4 × 3 = 300."],"trap":"Check: 6P4 = 360 total, minus 60 that start with 0 (5P3), gives 300.","answer":"300"},"mth-11-07-n06":{"idea":"Tie the two persons that must stand together into one block.","steps":["The block and the other 3 persons make 4 units.","4 units in a row: 4! = 24 ways.","The two persons in the block can swap: 2 ways.","Total = 24 × 2 = 48."],"trap":"Check: 5! = 120 in all, and 120 − 72 = 48 have the two apart.","answer":"48"},"mth-11-07-n07":{"idea":"Count all lines between pairs of vertices. Then remove the sides.","steps":["Pairs of vertices: 10C2 = (10 × 9)/2 = 45.","These 45 lines include the 10 sides.","Diagonals = 45 − 10 = 35."],"trap":"Check: n(n − 3)/2 = 10 × 7/2 = 35.","answer":"35"},"mth-11-07-n08":{"idea":"Write nC2 as a formula and solve the equation for n.","steps":["nC2 = n(n − 1)/2 = 45.","So n² − n − 90 = 0.","Factorise: (n − 10)(n + 9) = 0.","n cannot be negative, so n = 10."],"trap":"Check: 10C2 = 10 × 9/2 = 45.","answer":"10"},"mth-11-07-n09":{"idea":"Repeated letters must be divided out. Divide by the factorial of each repeat.","steps":["LEADER has 6 letters, and E appears twice.","If all were different: 6! = 720.","Divide by 2! for the two E's.","720/2 = 360."],"answer":"360"},"mth-11-07-n10":{"idea":"Count the routes step by step. Going and coming back are four steps in a row, so multiply. Routes cannot be reused.","steps":["Going: A to B has 3 routes, then B to C has 4 routes.","Coming back: C to B must use a route not used before, so 3 routes.","B to A must also avoid the route already used, so 2 routes.","Total = 3 × 4 × 3 × 2 = 72."],"trap":"The return routes are one fewer than the going routes.","answer":"72"}};
